steps.body:
Tailwind turns the usual way of writing CSS inside out, so before any class names, it's worth seeing what it's for. Recall the frameworks lesson: a utility class does one small job. Tailwind builds every piece of a page out of them, right in the HTML, and calls that **utility-first**.

## One class, one job

Each class sets one thing, usually a single CSS declaration:

| Class | What it sets |
| --- | --- |
| `p-4` | `padding: 1rem` |
| `mt-2` | `margin-top: 0.5rem` |
| `text-lg` | a larger font size, with a line height to match |
| `font-semibold` | `font-weight: 600` |
| `bg-blue-600` | a background color from Tailwind's palette |
| `rounded-lg` | `border-radius: 0.5rem` |
| `flex` | `display: flex` |
| `hidden` | `display: none` |

You style an element by listing the classes it needs, like `class="flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg p-4"`. There's no separate rule to write and no name to invent.

## Why teams like it

- **No naming.** Inventing names like `.card-header-inner` is slow, and they're never quite right. Here, nothing needs a name.
- **No switching files.** The styles sit on the element, so you see how it looks right where you edit it.
- **No dead CSS.** In a big stylesheet, nobody dares delete a rule in case something still uses it. With Tailwind, deleting the HTML deletes its styles, and the stylesheet never holds more than what's used.
- **A fixed scale.** `p-4` and `p-5` are steps on one spacing scale, and `blue-600` is one shade on one palette, so a team ends up with consistent spacing and color without a meeting about it.

## Why some don't

- Long lists of classes make the HTML noisier, and take getting used to.
- It's a new vocabulary to learn on top of CSS, even though the names map closely to it.
- The same list repeated on every button is hard to keep in step, unless you make each repeated piece a component of your own, as part 6 shows. In React, that comes naturally.

Next to the other two styles from the frameworks lesson: Bootstrap gives you finished pieces to adjust, and Tailwind gives you materials to build them from. Plain CSS sets the same properties, but in a stylesheet, from whatever scale you invent.

## How Tailwind writes the CSS

Tailwind doesn't ship a stylesheet with every class in it, since it would be enormous. Instead, it **scans** your project's files as plain text, picks out every word that could be a class name, and writes CSS only for the ones it recognizes.

```diagram tailwind-css-scan
Tailwind reads your files as text and writes rules only for the classes it finds.
```

In the diagram, Tailwind reads the HTML and the JavaScript in your project (step 1), keeps the words that are Tailwind classes (step 2), and writes a stylesheet with a rule for each one (step 3). Example 1 runs it by hand on the button from the frameworks lesson, and these are the rules it wrote for the button's classes:

```css
@layer utilities {
  .rounded-lg {
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }
  .bg-blue-600 {
    background-color: var(--color-blue-600);
  }
  .px-4 {
    padding-inline: calc(var(--spacing) * 4);
  }
  .py-2 {
    padding-block: calc(var(--spacing) * 2);
  }
  .font-semibold {
    --tw-font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
  }
  .text-white {
    color: var(--color-white);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .hover\:bg-blue-700:hover {
      background-color: var(--color-blue-700);
    }
  }
  .focus-visible\:outline-2:focus-visible {
    outline-style: var(--tw-outline-style);
    outline-width: 2px;
  }
  .focus-visible\:outline-offset-2:focus-visible {
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .focus-visible\:outline-blue-600:focus-visible {
    outline-color: var(--color-blue-600);
  }
}
```

Each class became one rule, and the values come from variables like `--spacing` and `--color-blue-600`, which part 5 explains. In a real project you don't run this by hand: Vite runs it on every save and every build, which the next part sets up.

Because the scan only reads text, a class has to appear in your files whole, exactly as it's written. Part 6 shows what goes wrong when it doesn't.

